{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 10, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "the social network is a masterful exploration of ambition, betrayal, and the origins of facebook. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the film moves at a breakneck pace, driven by sharp dialogue and a haunting score by trent reznor and atticus ross. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him not as a likable hero but as a complex, socially awkward genius whose drive for recognition alienates those closest to him. the narrative unfolds through dual storylines: the creation of the website and the subsequent legal battles with eduardo saverin and the winklevoss twins. andrew garfield shines as saverin, bringing emotional depth to the role of the betrayed best friend, while armie hammer effectively plays both cameron and tyler winklevoss. the cinematography is cool and detached, mirroring the emotional distance of the protagonist. rather than a simple biopic, the movie serves as a tragedy about friendship lost in the pursuit of success. it captures the zeitgeist of a generation connecting online while becoming increasingly isolated in real life. the final scene, with zuckerberg refreshing a profile page, leaves a lingering sense of emptiness that defines the entire experience. this is modern cinema at its finest, combining intellectual rigor with visceral storytelling.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.21483284}, {"token": " social", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " network", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.019491335}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -1.6865876}, {"token": "ful", "logprob": -0.007237755}, {"token": " exploration", "logprob": -3.601277}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ambition", "logprob": -3.5762793e-07}, {"token": ",", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " betrayal", "logprob": -1.549722e-06}, {"token": ",", "logpr